# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Files git should IGNORE (never upload to GitHub).
#
# If a file matches a line below, it stays on your computer only. This is how
# we keep your Supabase password off the public internet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Your secrets. This is the important one.
# .env holds your Supabase keys. Anyone with that file can read and delete
# your whole database, so it must never leave your machine.
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local

# The Python "virtual environment" — a private copy of Python and the
# libraries this project needs. It is thousands of files, it is rebuilt by
# `pip install -r requirements.txt` in seconds, and it is specific to your
# computer. No reason to upload it.
venv/
.venv/
env/

# Python's compiled cache. Generated automatically, meaningless to humans.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd

# macOS Finder litter.
.DS_Store

# Editor settings that are yours, not the project's.
.vscode/
.idea/

# Local scratch output (exports, debug HTML dumps, logs).
*.log
/tmp/
/out/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE: config/dealers.yml is deliberately NOT ignored.
#
# It ships with working example dealers and it is the file you edit to point
# the scraper at your own store. It contains only public website addresses —
# no passwords — so it is safe to commit, and committing it is what lets the
# GitHub Action know which sites to scrape on a schedule.
#
# If you ever put something private in it (an internal URL, a partner's site
# you would rather not publish), uncomment the next line to hide it from git:
#
# config/dealers.yml
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Your dashboard's settings file.
#
# dashboard/config.js holds your Supabase URL and your *anon* key. That key is
# read-only by design — sql/schema.sql grants it SELECT and nothing else — so
# this is nowhere near as dangerous as .env. But it is still a key, and it is
# specific to YOUR project, so it does not belong in a shared repo: anyone who
# cloned this would end up pointed at your database instead of their own.
#
# dashboard/config.example.js IS committed. That is the template people copy.
dashboard/config.js

# The dashboard's source code installs its build tools here (npm install).
# Hundreds of megabytes, rebuilt on demand, never committed. The BUILT site in
# dashboard/ (index.html + assets/) IS committed, so nobody needs Node to run it.
dashboard-src/node_modules/
